A client's CONNECTION_CLOSE during the handshake, as quic-go packs it.

quic-go's packConnectionClose writes the frame at every encryption level it still has a sealer for
— Initial, Handshake, 1-RTT, in that order — into one datagram, and pads the Initial so that the
datagram is maxPacketSize (vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:217-310, initialPaddingLen at :347-356);
the padding goes where appendPacketPayload puts it, after an ACK and before every other frame,
which for a close is first. A client drops its Initial keys once it has sent a Handshake packet,
so the Initial is there from the ServerHello until the client's own Finished — exactly when a
failed certificate or a TLS alert closes the connection. ngtcp2's client wrote the Handshake packet
alone, and an Initial close with its padding after the frame.

Anyone on the path can open an Initial packet: its keys come from the Destination Connection ID.

diff --git a/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c b/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
--- a/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
+++ b/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
@@ -5927,6 +5927,19 @@
